Subject: [PATCH 0/4] Share base memory across restored KVM sandboxes

This series lets restored sandboxes compose guest memory from one immutable
base image plus a private checkpoint delta. Multiple KVM sandboxes map the
same clean base pages MAP_PRIVATE, so the host page cache shares them until a
sandbox writes and receives its own copy-on-write page.

The save side compares committed pages with the base, records matching ranges
in checkpoint metadata, and omits those pages from the packed pages file. The
load side overlays the base before applying the remaining delta. Save and load
use the same walk order and skip set. In particular, a zero page over a
non-zero base page remains a real delta and is not omitted.

The runsc plumbing is opt-in. `checkpoint --shared-base` writes base.img and a
delta-only checkpoint. Restore discovers an optional base.img beside local
checkpoint files. Without the flag or base file, existing checkpoint and
restore behavior is unchanged.

Platform boundary
=================

This memory-sharing mechanism is correct for the KVM platform, where guest
execution consumes the sentry's composed MemoryFile mapping. It does not
provide shared-base memory on systrap: the systrap stub maps the per-sandbox
memfd separately and cannot see the sentry's MAP_PRIVATE overlay. Ordinary
checkpoint/restore remains unaffected on systrap.

Security boundary
=================

This series assumes base.img is an authenticated, immutable input. Fetching,
content verification, caching, and sealing belong to the node or orchestration
layer before runsc receives the file descriptor; no external verification
dependency is added to gVisor.

Results and validation
======================

The experiment suite was run on Linux/KVM using gVisor at base commit
928199eb9fb28593a69ac3daffdba01c3319dc8f:

  * pgalloc base/delta tests passed through synchronous and asynchronous load;
  * controlled tests measured 4.9x to 15x Rss/Pss flatten;
  * eight live KVM sandboxes over a 256 MiB base measured 5.69x flatten;
  * a Python ADK workload restored correctly and measured 2.9x flatten over an
    80 MiB resident base, limited by about 20 MiB of per-sandbox overhead.

The reconstructed four-commit series is tree-identical to the combined patch
used for those runs. `git diff --check` passes. A local Darwin Bazel build
cannot validate the Linux-only target: unchanged gVisor code fails while
compiling pkg/sentry/usage because memutil.CreateMemFD is unavailable on
Darwin. Re-run `bazel test //pkg/sentry/pgalloc:pgalloc_test` on Linux before
applying the series in production.

Repeated restore-dirty-checkpoint generations have not yet been exercised end
to end. The checkpoint-after-restore composition is covered at the MemoryFile
layer, but should receive a live KVM lifecycle test.

Background and reproduction harness:
https://zt.dev/posts/gvisor-shared-memory-snapshot-restore/

